A||N||7||CraigC||Hard Shifting||||1||
Z||000000||CraigC||11-23-2001||10:52 PM||||Just purchased an 84 with the 2.5l cylinder, 100,000 miles. This is from a one time owner, the car came complete with a GM service manual, original window sticker price sheet, articles from 1984 Road & Track and every receipt from the date of purchase for all and any work that was completed. I purchased this car for $500.00 Canadian and it is in awesome condition. I am experiencing some stiff shifting (knew this when I bought it)Mainly between 1st and 2nd gears. This happens wether the car is cold or warm and not all the time. Does it require Clutch fluid change or should I be prepared to spend some bucks, nothing is lost so far! Has been fun to drive so far. Any opinions please feed them my way||||reg||1
Z||000001||aubicon||11-23-2001||11:15 PM||||you might want to check the shift and select cables, on my 87 gt the selector cable was bad which made for hard shifting. just an idia.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Gary W||11-23-2001||11:30 PM||||Check the manual for cable adjustment procedure. Also, look for slop in the selector arm bracket - the bushings are plastic and wear out. Clutch fluid should only be a factor if you're having a problem getting it in first, or there's a problem with pedal travel.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Turbo88||11-23-2001||11:36 PM||||I've found several things that can cause this. Like aubicon said, it could be worn shift and select cables. It could be air in the clutch system. Perhaps leaky seals in the master or slave. It could even be a worn clutch peddle. <P>I have a different transmission but it might be a simular problem. In my Getrag 5sp, I found it hard to shift into first. Then harder into first and hard into second. It got progressively worse until I could not shift into first or second at all. Turned out that the transmission was burnt up. It had developed a leak in the transmission and slowly lost it's fluid. Being dumb and too busy to care about it at the time, I never bothered to check the fluid level in the transaxle. Just some things you might want to check. Hope this helps...<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Eriamjh||11-24-2001||09:21 AM||||Drain the transmission and put synthetic oil in it for starters.<P>Second, bleed your clutch system.<P>Third: Your cables might have stretched.  <P>Fourth: Clutch might need replacing.<P>Fifth: Uh... what they said, too!<P>------------------<BR>Dan||||reg||1
Z||000005||Mickey_Moose||11-24-2001||11:15 AM||||missing clip on the tansmission that holds the cables to the case?!? I have seen this many times with members in our club - missing clip or a loose one.<P>Tim<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Rrazzz||11-24-2001||08:27 PM||||1.  I get the impression that a good way to determine if the shift problem is with the clutch or with the shifter/transmission is to try shifting w/ engine not running. If shifting if much easier with engine off then shift problem is probably due to clutch not fully releasing.<BR>So others agree?<P>2.  Changing trans fluid and flushing clutch/brake fluid are good maintenance even if they may not be related to current problem.||||reg||1
Z||000007||SLYGUY||11-24-2001||10:03 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Turbo88:<P><B>It could be air in the clutch system. <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That was the case for me. You could either:<BR>1) Spend hours and hours with a close personal friend to help you bleed the system or<BR>2) Buy a Mityvac brake bleeder and much less time/inconvenience bleeding it that way.<P>Good luck!! <BR>Sly||||reg||1
